The thing is, though, that it violates *pre-ANSI* C as well. It's all 
well and good to make every implementation defined response emit the 
text "I want a chocolate bar" to the console if that's what the vendor 
chooses. But the lower limits of the integer types have never been 
considered implementation defined. They were formally specified in 
whatever the most official documentation happened to be at any point in 
time.

On those grounds I assert that, despite the name, the product you've 
identified is simply not a C compiler. It's a compiler for a C-like 
language for a specific range of hardware that can't reasonably be 
targeted by a full, compliant C implementation. It's not just the 
integer size issue; on a quick skim of a handful of pages around what 
you cited, I saw other deviations in behavior that really make this 
nothing like compatible with an "actual" C compiler.

